1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,709
Welcome to Atomic Education.

2
00:00:02,770 --> 00:00:10,153
This lesson was written by the Atomic teaching team to help you learn Cambridge Lower Secondary Science with confidence.

3
00:00:10,153 --> 00:00:10,885
Let's begin!

4
00:00:11,947 --> 00:00:14,204
Welcome to Unit 8: Forces and Motion.

5
00:00:15,265 --> 00:00:19,232
Kicking a football, swinging a cricket bat, a rocket taking off,

6
00:00:19,232 --> 00:00:22,832
a car braking at the lights: every action involves forces.

7
00:00:22,832 --> 00:00:27,470
Transport produces about one-fifth of the world's carbon dioxide emissions.

8
00:00:27,470 --> 00:00:32,351
Understanding forces and motion can help us design more fuel-efficient vehicles.

9
00:00:33,412 --> 00:00:38,904
Speed tells us how fast something is moving: the distance travelled in each unit of time.

10
00:00:38,904 --> 00:00:44,274
People walk at about 1 metre per second, run at about 3, a car might go 15, a train 30,

11
00:00:44,274 --> 00:00:49,400
and a plane about 250 metres per second! An object that isn't moving has zero speed.

12
00:00:50,461 --> 00:00:56,746
To calculate speed, divide the distance travelled by the time taken. The SI unit is metres per second.

13
00:00:56,746 --> 00:01:00,041
For longer journeys, we often use kilometres per hour.

14
00:01:01,102 --> 00:01:05,008
Let's try it. An Olympic athlete runs 100 metres in 10 seconds.

15
00:01:05,008 --> 00:01:09,157
Speed equals 100 metres divided by 10 seconds: 10 metres per second.

16
00:01:10,218 --> 00:01:15,405
Speed matters everywhere in daily life. Here are four places where people measure it.

17
00:01:16,466 --> 00:01:20,738
Walking to school, most people move at about 1.4 metres every second,

18
00:01:20,738 --> 00:01:23,423
so one kilometre takes about twelve minutes.

19
00:01:24,484 --> 00:01:26,802
Near schools, speed limits are lower.

20
00:01:26,802 --> 00:01:31,867
A slower car travels less distance while the driver reacts, so it can stop in time.

21
00:01:32,928 --> 00:01:36,651
On sports day, a 100 metre race can be timed electronically.

22
00:01:36,651 --> 00:01:40,007
Speed is the distance divided by the time on the clock.

23
00:01:41,068 --> 00:01:47,597
Airline pilots work out how long a flight takes by dividing the distance by the average speed of the plane.

24
00:01:48,658 --> 00:01:53,357
In every example, speed is the distance travelled divided by the time taken.

25
00:01:53,357 --> 00:01:56,652
Pause and think of one more place where speed matters.

26
00:01:57,713 --> 00:02:03,632
We can rearrange the speed equation using a speed triangle. Cover the quantity you want to find.

27
00:02:03,632 --> 00:02:08,270
Distance equals speed times time, and time equals distance divided by speed.

28
00:02:09,331 --> 00:02:14,273
A car travels 150 kilometres at 50 kilometres per hour. How long is the journey?

29
00:02:14,273 --> 00:02:17,752
Cover t: time equals 150 divided by 50, which is 3 hours.

30
00:02:18,813 --> 00:02:20,643
Units really matter. In 1999,

31
00:02:20,643 --> 00:02:26,135
NASA's Mars Climate Orbiter crashed because one team calculated thrust in imperial units,

32
00:02:26,135 --> 00:02:31,322
and another in metric units. The spacecraft flew too close to Mars and was destroyed.

33
00:02:32,383 --> 00:02:35,495
An athlete doesn't run at the same speed all race.

34
00:02:35,495 --> 00:02:41,780
At the start, she accelerates: she covers more distance each second, 5 metres, then 6, then 7, then 8.

35
00:02:41,780 --> 00:02:46,784
At the end, she decelerates: she covers less distance each second before stopping.

36
00:02:47,845 --> 00:02:51,689
So we use average speed: total distance divided by total time.

37
00:02:51,689 --> 00:02:56,205
In the first three seconds, she runs 5 plus 6 plus 7, which is 18 metres.

38
00:02:56,205 --> 00:03:00,904
18 metres divided by 3 seconds gives an average speed of 6 metres per second.

39
00:03:01,965 --> 00:03:04,650
How do police know if a driver is speeding?

40
00:03:04,650 --> 00:03:09,470
A radar speed camera sends out radio waves, which bounce off a moving vehicle.

41
00:03:09,470 --> 00:03:13,620
The camera uses the returning waves to work out the vehicle's speed.

42
00:03:14,681 --> 00:03:21,271
A two-point camera system photographs a vehicle at two places a known distance apart and records the times.

43
00:03:21,271 --> 00:03:24,322
Then it calculates the average speed between them.

44
00:03:25,383 --> 00:03:27,641
In the lab, we can use timing gates.

45
00:03:27,641 --> 00:03:33,316
A card on a toy car breaks an infrared beam, which starts and stops a timer very accurately.

46
00:03:33,316 --> 00:03:39,357
Races are timed electronically to a thousandth of a second, much more accurately than a stopwatch,

47
00:03:39,357 --> 00:03:41,615
which depends on human reaction time.
